[[Next->Q7]]


:: F7 {"position":"525,150","size":"100,100"}
Model answer: C: It allows the electron transport chain to function

Oxygen serves as the terminal electron acceptor in aerobic respiration. By accepting electrons, it enables continued operation of the electron transport chain and oxidative phosphorylation. Glycolysis can occur without oxygen, and oxygen is not a glycogen component nor involved in converting ATP to glucose.

