[[Next->Q8]]


:: F8 {"position":"775,150","size":"100,100"}
Model answer: C: Humans possess enzymes that hydrolyse alpha(1→4) linkages but not beta(1→4) linkages

Human digestive enzymes efficiently hydrolyse alpha(1→4) glycosidic bonds in starch but lack cellulases that cleave beta(1→4) bonds in cellulose. The difference in linkage chemistry determines digestibility. Nitrogen content, stomach entry and simple energy content do not explain the distinction.

